# Part 4 of Our Common Bond: Australian values (the five values questions on the real test come from here)
CAT = "aucitValues"; SEG = "VAL"; PER_TEST = 5
Q = [
("Australian values are based on which ideas?",
 "Freedom, respect and fairness", ["Wealth, status and family name", "Loyalty to one official religion", "Obedience to community elders"],
 "Our Common Bond says Australian values are based on freedom, respect, fairness and equality of opportunity, and keep the community secure, prosperous and peaceful."),
("Under the Rule of Law, when should you obey the law?",
 "Even when no one is watching", ["Only when police are nearby", "Only if you agree with it", "Only in public places"],
 "Everybody should obey the law at all times, even if no one is watching, or face penalties. No person or group is above the law."),
("What does compulsory voting show about Australian values?",
 "Taking part in elections matters", ["Fines raise money for government", "One party should stay in power", "Voting counts the population"],
 "Voting is compulsory, which shows the importance of participating in elections in a parliamentary democracy."),
("Ana strongly disagrees with a new law. Which action fits Australian values?",
 "Protest peacefully within the law", ["Refuse to obey the law", "Threaten her local MP", "Block the MP's office by force"],
 "Tolerance of peaceful public protest is an essential part of democracy, but protests must be within the law and the law must still be obeyed."),
("Is it acceptable to promote violence against a group because of their religion or culture?",
 "No, never", ["Yes, if it is posted online", "Yes, during a protest", "Only if the group started it"],
 "It is never acceptable to promote violence against a person or group because of their culture, ethnicity, religion or background. It is against Australian values and the law."),
("Which of these is illegal, even with freedom of speech?",
 "Making false allegations", ["Criticising the Prime Minister", "Writing to a newspaper", "Joining a peaceful protest"],
 "Freedom of speech has limits: it is illegal to make false allegations or encourage others to break the law. Criticising leaders and peaceful protest are allowed."),
("Do newspapers, television and radio in Australia share in freedom of speech?",
 "Yes, within the law", ["No, the government approves news", "Only state-owned outlets do", "Only during election campaigns"],
 "Newspapers, television and radio have the same freedom to express ideas, as long as it is within the law."),
("Sam's relatives want him to join their community group. Can he be made to join?",
 "No, joining must be voluntary", ["Yes, if he lives with them", "Yes, until he turns 21", "Yes, if the group is legal"],
 "Under freedom of association people join or leave groups voluntarily, and no one can be compelled to join an organisation."),
("Can a person leave a group they no longer agree with?",
 "Yes, they are free to leave", ["Only with the group's consent", "Only after paying a fine", "No, membership is for life"],
 "People are free to join or leave any group voluntarily, and no one can be forced to stay in, or to leave, an organisation."),
("What legal status do religious laws have in Australia?",
 "None", ["They override state laws", "They bind their followers", "They equal federal law"],
 "Religious laws have no legal status in Australia. Everyone must follow Australian law, even where it differs from religious law."),
("A married man wants to marry a second wife. What does Australian law say?",
 "Polygamy is against the law", ["It is legal if his religion allows", "It is legal with his wife's consent", "It is legal after a waiting period"],
 "Being married to more than one person at the same time is against the law and can bring severe penalties, whatever a person's religion or culture."),
("Parents arrange for their daughter to marry a man she has not freely agreed to. What is this?",
 "A forced marriage, which is illegal", ["A cultural practice the law allows", "Legal if she is over 16", "Legal if both families agree"],
 "A forced marriage, where one or both people do not have a free choice, is against the law and can lead to imprisonment."),
("Is religious intolerance acceptable in Australian society?",
 "No, it is not acceptable", ["Yes, towards newer faiths", "Yes, if no violence is used", "Only towards non-citizens"],
 "Religious intolerance is not acceptable. Everyone should have equal opportunity whatever their ethnicity or religion, as long as they obey the law."),
("How are Buddhists and people of other faiths treated under Australian law?",
 "The same as Christians", ["With fewer legal rights", "With extra legal rights", "As a separate legal group"],
 "The law gives no one preferential treatment or discrimination because of background; Buddhists and people of all other faiths are treated the same as Christians."),
("Under Australian law, can two men or two women marry each other?",
 "Yes", ["No", "Only in some states", "Only if married overseas"],
 "Under Australian law two people can marry each other, including two men or two women."),
("Which rights do women have compared with men in Australia?",
 "Equal rights", ["Fewer rights at work", "Rights set by their husband", "Rights set by their religion"],
 "Men and women have equal rights and should have equal opportunity to pursue their goals, including in education, work, voting and the courts."),
("Maria has better qualifications and skills than a male applicant for a job. Who should get it?",
 "Maria", ["The male applicant", "The older applicant", "The applicant with more money"],
 "It is a right for a woman to get a job ahead of a man if she has better qualifications and skills. Jobs should go to the person with the best skills and experience."),
("Can either a husband or a wife apply for a divorce?",
 "Yes, either spouse can", ["Only the husband can", "Only if both agree", "Only with a religious leader's approval"],
 "Divorce is acceptable in Australia, and either spouse may apply to the courts, even if the other wishes to stay married."),
("Is physical violence against a spouse or partner ever acceptable?",
 "No, it is a criminal offence", ["Yes, if it is a private matter", "Yes, in some cultures", "Only if the partner agrees"],
 "Physical violence against a spouse or partner is never acceptable and is a criminal offence in Australia."),
("Who makes decisions about personal matters such as marriage and religion?",
 "Each person for themselves", ["Their parents", "Their spouse", "Their community leaders"],
 "Both men and women have the right to make their own independent choices about personal matters such as marriage and religion, and the law protects them from intimidation."),
("When an organisation fills a job, what does the law support?",
 "Choosing the most skilled person", ["Choosing a family member", "Choosing someone of the same faith", "Choosing the wealthiest applicant"],
 "The law supports selecting the person with the best skills and experience, not choosing because of gender, wealth or ethnicity."),
("Many new migrants have become leaders in business, the arts and sport. Our Common Bond credits this to what?",
 "Hard work and enterprise", ["Family wealth from overseas", "Government appointments", "Their religious connections"],
 "A fair go means success comes from hard work and talent, and many migrants have become leaders through their hard work and enterprise."),
("Is verbal abuse of another person acceptable in Australia?",
 "No, it is a form of violence", ["Yes, if no one is touched", "Yes, within a family", "Yes, if it is meant as a joke"],
 "Violence of any kind, including verbal and physical abuse, is illegal. Australians believe in the right to be safe from violence and intimidation."),
("What is the age of sexual consent in Australia?",
 "16 or 17, by state or territory", ["14 in every state and territory", "15 or 16, by state or territory", "18 in every state and territory"],
 "The age of consent is 16 or 17 depending on the state or territory, and the laws are very strict."),
("A police officer makes a lawful request of you. What should you do?",
 "Obey it", ["Ignore it if you disagree", "Obey only if you are a citizen", "Ask a community leader first"],
 "The lawful actions of police should be supported, and you should obey a lawful request because all Australians commit to following the law."),
("What does mutual respect mean in Australia?",
 "Listening to views you disagree with", ["Agreeing with the majority view", "Avoiding people who hold different views", "Keeping your opinions to yourself"],
 "Mutual respect and tolerance mean listening to others and respecting their views even when they differ from your own."),
("A fan shouts racially abusive comments at a sports match. What do Australian values say?",
 "Racism has no place in Australia", ["It is allowed at sporting events", "It is allowed if meant as a joke", "It is a private matter for fans"],
 "Racism has no place in Australia, including racially abusive comments in a public place or at a sporting event and racist material online."),
("Apart from the police, where can incidents of racism be reported?",
 "The Australian Human Rights Commission", ["The Australian Electoral Commission office", "The Australian Taxation Office", "The Department of Defence"],
 "The Australian Human Rights Commission is an independent body that investigates complaints about discrimination and human rights breaches, including racism."),
("What do Australians expect of everyone living in Australia?",
 "To contribute to the community", ["To live only with their own group", "To rely fully on government support", "To stay out of local affairs"],
 "Australians expect everyone to participate in society and make a contribution to the community."),
("Which responsibility does everyone have when they are able to?",
 "Supporting themselves and their family", ["Joining a political party", "Living close to their extended relatives", "Joining the Defence Force"],
 "Everyone has a responsibility to try to support themselves and their families when they are able to do so."),
("What is \"mateship\"?",
 "Helping each other in times of need", ["Loyalty only to your old friends", "Choosing friends of your background", "A legal duty to share property"],
 "Mateship means helping and receiving help from others, especially in difficult times. It is part of the value of compassion for those in need."),
("Your elderly neighbour is unwell and lives alone. Which action shows mateship?",
 "Taking them a meal", ["Reporting them to the council", "Telling relatives to move them", "Leaving them alone to rest"],
 "Our Common Bond gives taking a meal to an elderly neighbour or visiting someone sick, frail or lonely as examples of mateship."),
("Why does Australia have a strong tradition of volunteering?",
 "To look out for each other", ["Because the law requires it", "To earn a tax refund", "To qualify for citizenship"],
 "In the spirit of mateship Australians volunteer to look out for each other and strengthen the community. It is encouraged, not required."),
("Why is English valued as Australia's national language?",
 "It is a unifying element", ["It is the only language allowed", "It replaced Aboriginal languages", "It must be spoken at home"],
 "English is valued as an important unifying element of society and is essential for economic participation and social cohesion. Other languages are still valued."),
("What must citizenship applicants undertake about English, if it is not their native language?",
 "Make reasonable efforts to learn it", ["Pass an advanced English exam", "Stop using their first language entirely", "Speak only English at home"],
 "Applicants must undertake to make reasonable efforts to learn English if it is not their native language."),
("How does learning English help people living in Australia?",
 "With education, jobs and community life", ["It removes the need to pay tax", "It shortens the wait for a passport by a year", "It allows voting before age 18"],
 "Speaking English helps people get an education and a job and integrate into the community."),
("You suspect a neighbour is planning a serious crime. What should you do?",
 "Report it to the authorities", ["Warn the neighbour first", "Wait until it happens", "Tell only your own family"],
 "If people suspect someone may be planning a serious crime they should report it to Australian government authorities as soon as possible."),
("If you know a child is being abused, who should you report it to?",
 "The police", ["The child's school only", "A religious leader", "No one, it is private"],
 "Anyone who sees or knows of a child being abused should report it to the police to investigate."),
("Which of these is an example of cyber abuse?",
 "Sharing sexual photos without consent", ["Posting your own holiday photos publicly", "Criticising a policy online", "Writing a bad restaurant review"],
 "Cyber abuse includes sharing sexual photos or videos without consent, stalking someone online and racially abusive comments. Many forms are illegal."),
("Can an Australian citizen also hold citizenship of another country?",
 "Yes, if that country allows it", ["No, never", "Only if born in Australia", "Only for the first five years"],
 "Australian citizens may hold dual or multiple citizenship if the laws of the other countries allow it."),
("Kim is a dual citizen living in Australia. Which laws must she follow?",
 "All Australian laws, at all times", ["Only her other country's laws", "Only the laws she agrees with", "Australian laws except family law"],
 "Even if a person is also a citizen of another country, an Australian citizen in Australia must follow all Australian laws at all times."),
("Which Australian law still applies to Australians when they are overseas?",
 "No sexual activity with a child under 16", ["Australian road rules", "The rules on alcohol in licensed venues", "Local council parking rules"],
 "Some Australian laws apply overseas too. It is illegal for Australians to be involved in any sexual activity, here or overseas, with a child under 16."),
("Sharing official government secrets on social media would do what?",
 "Undermine Australia's interests", ["Show freedom of speech", "Help keep the community safe", "Uphold the Rule of Law"],
 "Australians share an obligation not to undermine Australia's interests and security, and sharing official secrets would do that."),
("What would promoting distrust and fear of an ethnic community do?",
 "Damage community relations", ["Protect national security", "Support freedom of association", "Help new migrants integrate"],
 "Promoting distrust in and fear of an ethnic community damages Australian community relations and goes against the obligation not to undermine Australia's interests."),
("During the 2019-20 bushfires, how did many multicultural communities respond?",
 "They collected donations to help", ["They left the affected areas", "They waited for the government", "They held protests in the cities"],
 "Many communities collected money, clothes, food and water for people and firefighters affected by the bushfires, showing how Australians come together in a crisis."),
("Equality under the law means people from different backgrounds are:",
 "Neither favoured nor discriminated against", ["Given special help based on their religion", "Judged by their homeland's laws", "Judged by their community elders"],
 "The law is applied so that people from different backgrounds are not given preferential treatment, nor discriminated against."),
("A shop owner refuses to hire a woman only because she is a woman. Under Australian law, this is:",
 "Illegal discrimination", ["Allowed for small businesses", "Allowed for cultural reasons", "A private business choice"],
 "It is against the law to discriminate against a person because of their gender. Jobs should go on skills and experience."),
("Jacob says Australian law does not apply to him because of his culture. Is he right?",
 "No, Australian laws apply to all", ["Yes, if his culture is very old", "Yes, until he becomes a citizen", "Yes, inside his own home"],
 "Australian laws apply to all people in Australia, whatever their background or culture."),
("Can both men and women vote at elections and stand for parliament?",
 "Yes, both have equal rights", ["Only men can stand for election", "Only if they are married", "Women need a male sponsor"],
 "Men and women can both vote, stand for parliament, join the Defence Force and police, and are treated equally in courts."),
("How do Australians believe disagreements should be handled?",
 "Peacefully", ["With force if needed", "By avoiding each other", "By a religious ruling"],
 "Australians believe in peaceful disagreement and, above all, the right to be safe and free from violence and intimidation."),
]
